 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define a regex pattern to match adjective phrases (optional article + adjective + noun)
  const adjectivePattern = /^([article]\s*)?((\w+)\s+(adjective|JJ\.|\b[A-Z][a-z]*[JJ]\s+)|[A-Z][a-z]+[-\']?[A-Z][a-z]+\s+)[a-z]+(([,\.!\?]\s*)|$)/g;

  let matches = [];
  let lastMatchIndex = -1;
  let currentMatch;

  // Find all adjective phrases in the sentence
  while ((currentMatch = adjectivePattern.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    matches.push(currentMatch[0]);
    lastMatchIndex = RegExp.lastIndex;
  }

  // Reconstruct the sentence with switched adjective order
  const result = [];
  for (let i = matches.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    result.unshift(matches[i]);
  }

  // Insert the reconstructed adjective phrases back into the original sentence
  const words = sentence.split(' ');
  let index = 0;
  for (const phrase of result) {
    words.splice(index, 0, phrase);
    index += phrase.match(/[\w'-]+/.length) + 1;
  }

  return words.join(' ') + ' ';
}
// Test the function
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park")); // "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```
This function defines a regex pattern to match adjective phrases, finds them in the input sentence, switches their order, and then inserts them back into the sentence at the correct positions. Note that this function assumes that each adjective phrase consists of an optional article, one or more adjectives, and a following noun

