while ~feof(fid1) && ~feof(fid2)
    line1 = fgetl(fid1);
    line2 = fgetl(fid2);
    words1Temp = strsplit(lower(line1), '\s+');
    words2Temp = strsplit(lower(line2), '\s+');

    % Combine words1 and words2 cell arrays
    words1 = [words1; words1Temp];
    words2 = [words2; words2Temp];
end
% Close input files
fclose(fid1);
fclose(fid2);
% Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);
% Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```
This implementation reads the files line by line and only stores the words in cell arrays, which is more memory-efficient than loading the entire text into memory at once. It should be able to handle very large texts as long as there is enough available memory to store the combined words cell array.

