 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, you can use the `JwtSecurityToken` class from the `System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt` namespace to decode and parse the JWT token. Here's how to do it:

1. Install the Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens NuGet package if you haven't already:
   ```
   Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
   ```

2. Create a helper method in your controller or utility class to extract the username from the JWT token:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwt(string jwtToken)
{
    try
    {
        // Parse the JWT token
        JwtSecurityToken handler = new JwtSecurityToken(jwtToken);

        // Get the username claim value
        ClaimsPrincipal claimsPrincipal = new ClaimsPrincipal(handler.Claims);
        return claimsPrincipal.FindFirst("sub").Value;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new BadRequestObjectResult($"Error parsing JWT token: {ex.Message}");
    }
}
```

3. Use the helper method in your action methods as needed:

```csharp
[HttpGet]
public IActionResult GetUserData([FromHeader] string AuthorizationHeader)
{
    if (!String.IsNullOrEmpty(AuthorizationHeader))
    {
        // Remove Bearer prefix from the header
        String token = AuthorizationHeader.Replace("Bearer ", "");

        // Retrieve username from JWT token
        string username = GetUsernameFromJwt(token);

        // Your implementation for getting user data based on the username
        // ...
    }
    else
    {
        return Unauthorized();
    }
}
```

This way, you can easily access the username from the JWT token in your ASP.NET C# WebApi actions. Keep in mind that this example assumes you have set up proper authentication and authorization mechanisms to validate and handle incoming JWT tokens.

 To design an efficient NoSQL document schema for a hotel room reservation system on MongoDB based on the given information, I suggest the following schema:

```javascript
{
  "hotelProperty": {
    "_id": ObjectId, // Unique identifier for each hotel property
    "name": String,
    "location": {
      "address": String,
      "city": String,
      "country": String
    },
    "rooms": [
      {
        "_id": ObjectId, // Unique identifier for each room
        "number": Number,
        "type": String,
        "capacity": {
          "maxAdults": Number,
          "maxChildren": Number
        },
        "availability": {
          "$": { // Use MongoDB's positional filtering operator $ to update availability dynamically
            "lastUpdated": {
              type: Date,
              default: new Date()
            },
            "reservations": [
              {
                "_id": ObjectId, // Unique identifier for each reservation
                "checkIn": ISODate,
                "checkOut": ISODate,
                "guests": [
                  {
                    "adultName": String,
                    "childName": String,
                    "age": Number
                  }
                ],
                "status": String // (e.g., 'booked', 'cancelled', 'pending')
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

This schema consists of a single top-level document containing a list of hotel properties. Each hotel property document includes its name, location, and a list of room documents. Each room document contains its number, type, capacity, and availability. Availability is represented as a subdocument with a positional filtering operator `$` to allow dynamic updates to the reservations array. This design allows for efficient querying and updating of hotel properties and their respective rooms' availability.

