 To prevent the planets from being generated too close to each other, you can modify the `generateRandomPlanets` function by keeping track of the minimum distances between the newly generated planet and all existing planets. Here's an updated version of your function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [] as Array<{
    position: number[];
    size: number;
    color: string;
    ringSize: number;
    ringColor: string;
    ringNoise: number;
  }>;
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    let tooClose = false;

    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const existingPlanet = planets[i];
      const distanceSquared = planet.position.map((_, index) => {
        const diff = Math.pow(planet.position[index] - existingPlanet.position[index], 2);
        return diff;
      }).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);

      if (distanceSquared < Math.pow(minDistance, 2)) {
        tooClose = true;
        break;
      }
    }

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
}
```

This updated version checks the minimum distance between the new planet and all existing planets using the Euclidean distance formula. If the distance is less than the minimum distance, it considers the new planet as too close and continues generating new planets until it finds a suitable one.

 To create a React utility for removing widows, we can write a custom Hook called `useNoWidows`. This Hook will take in a string as an argument and return an object containing the modified lines with no widows. A widow is the last line of a paragraph that appears alone on a new line when a page is broken at the end of the line. Here's how you can implement it:

```javascript
import { useMemo } from 'react';

const LINE_BREAK_REGEX = /(?<=[^.\?!])\s+(?=\.|\?|!)(\s+)/g;

function getModifiedLines(text) {
  const lines = text.split('\n');
  let lastLineEndIndex = -1;
  const modifiedLines = [];

  lines.forEach((line, index) => {
    if (index === lines.length - 1) {
      // Handle the last line
      const match = LINE_BREAK_REGEX.exec(line);
      if (match && match[0].length > 0) {
        // If there is an extra space before a period or question mark, remove it
        const modifiedLastLine = line.slice(0, match.index) + line.slice(match.index + match[0].length);
        modifiedLines.push(modifiedLastLine);
        return;
      }

      // Add the last line without modifications
      modifiedLines.push(line);
      return;
    }

    // Handle other lines
    modifiedLines.push(line);

    // Update lastLineEndIndex for the next line
    lastLineEndIndex = line.length;
  });

  return modifiedLines;
}

export function useNoWidows(text) {
  const modifiedLines = useMemo(() => getModifiedLines(text), [text]);
  return modifiedLines;
}
```

